## Abstract

Module-Lattice-Based Key-Encapsulation Mechanisms, standardized in NIST FIPS 203 (ML-KEM), rely heavily on the Number Theoretic Transform (NTT) for polynomial multiplication over the ring $R_q = \mathbb{Z}_q[X]/(X^{256} + 1)$ with modulus $q = 3329$. In resource-constrained and high-reliability environments, such as aerospace avionics, terrestrial edge controllers, and safety-critical embedded systems, computational hardware is exposed to transient and persistent faults caused by environmental disturbances, voltage fluctuations, and thermal stress. In this work, we investigate architectural fault resilience against transient and persistent register perturbations. Unmitigated computational faults during decapsulation risk inducing Silent Data Corruption (SDC) or triggering unnecessary algorithm aborts that degrade communication availability. 

In this paper, we design, implement, and empirically validate an end-to-end software-level fault-resilience architecture for ML-KEM-768. Our scheme continuously monitors all 11 polynomial transforms (6 Forward Cooley-Tukey and 5 Inverse Gentleman-Sande, totaling exactly $9{,}856$ butterflies) executed during decapsulation and Fujisaki-Okamoto (FO) re-encryption. By combining localized single-butterfly register snapshotting with decoupled algebraic invariant checking, transient arithmetic errors are detected and corrected in-place via bounded local rollback, requiring only a single-butterfly recomputation ($0.01\%$ execution overhead per fault) rather than full decapsulation restarts. Persistent faults safely escalate to fail-stop containment, forcing FIPS 203 Section 7.3 implicit rejection. 

We conduct extensive fault-injection experiments directly on compiled production binaries:
1. Under transient multiplier upsets ($F4$), the system achieves a $100.0\%$ recovery rate ($100/100$, $95\%$ Wilson score CI: $[96.30\%, 100.00\%]$), successfully emitting the authentic shared secret key $K_{\text{valid}}$.
2. Under persistent faults ($F5A$) and software force-value zeroing ($F5B$), the system achieves $100.0\%$ safe fail-stop containment ($200/200$, $95\%$ CI: $[98.14\%, 100.00\%]$), deterministically escalating to implicit rejection $K_{\text{rej}} = \text{SHAKE256}(z \parallel c)$.
3. Across 300 active production fault trials on compiled binaries, zero Silent Data Corruption (SDC) events were observed ($0.00\%$, $95\%$ Wilson score CI: $[0.00\%, 1.26\%]$), consistent with the $0/750$ SDC rate (one-sided $97.5\%$ Clopper-Pearson upper bound: $0.49\%$) observed across the broader 900-trial canonical campaign.
4. We evaluate common-mode failure boundaries: while circular verifiers that consume unshielded live outputs exhibit a $98.5\%$ false-pass rate ($591/600$, with individual test configurations spanning $94.0\%$ to $100.0\%$), snapshot-isolated checking maintains full detection. Furthermore, we mathematically prove that local arithmetic invariants are intrinsically blind to twiddle factor memory corruption ($0.0\%$ local detection), while an exploratory 11-trial active mutation pilot confirms that downstream FO ciphertext re-encryption comparison catches the resulting polynomial corruption, deterministically returning the implicit rejection key on the evaluated reference vector.

Decapsulation performance benchmarks on an Apple Silicon ARM64 processor (Apple M3 Pro, macOS Darwin 25.1.0, Apple Clang 17.0.0) demonstrate an empirical latency overhead of $+16.9\%$ under `-O3` ($129.51\,\mu\text{s}$ vs. $110.77\,\mu\text{s}$, $N = 2{,}000$)—substantially lower than sequential software dual modular redundancy (DMR), which conceptually requires at least $+100.0\%$ ($2.0\times T_0$) latency overhead plus comparison costs. The monitoring implementation requires zero dynamic heap allocations and preserves constant-time execution during fault-free operation.

**Keywords:** Post-Quantum Cryptography, ML-KEM-768, FIPS 203, Number Theoretic Transform (NTT), Fault Injection, Algebraic Invariants, Localized Rollback, Implicit Rejection, Space Systems.

