## 5. Experimental Methodology

### 5.1 Test Environment and Reproduction Harness

Empirical decapsulation latency benchmarks reported in Table 4 were recorded on an Apple Silicon ARM64 processor (Apple M3 Pro, 12 CPU cores, macOS Darwin 25.1.0) using Apple Clang 17.0.0 (`-O3`) across $N = 2{,}000$ warm-stabilized iterations (`experiments/phase7i/phase7i_e2e_validation_harness.c`). Binary reproduction harnesses and functional validations were evaluated under macOS Darwin 27.0.0 using `Apple clang version 21.0.0 (clang-2100.1.1.101)`. Because per-iteration timing records were not persisted to disk, summary dispersion metrics reflect harness stdout aggregations, and we distinguish historical M3 Pro benchmark records from local M1 execution environments rather than asserting a single uniform platform. The reproduction harness is compiled directly against production object files:
```bash
clang -O3 -Wall -Wextra \
  -Ialgorithms/source_ref/include -Ialgorithms/source_ref/cpa -Ialgorithms/source_ref/kem \
  phase18_audit/production_reproducer.c bin/obj/mlkem768_inst.o bin/obj/mlkem_fault_hooks.o \
  -o phase18_audit/production_reproducer
```

### 5.2 Fault Models and Injection Mechanisms

We evaluated four primary fault targets across 400 clean production trials:
1. **$F12$ (Baseline Control):** Zero faults injected. Validates baseline algorithmic correctness and absence of false-alarm aborts ($N = 100$).
2. **$F4$ (Transient Multiplier SEU):** A single-bit bitflip injected into the multiplier product $t$ during the execution of a randomly selected butterfly stage ($N = 100$). The fault is transient, clearing immediately on subsequent cycles.
3. **$F5A$ (Persistent Multiplier Fault):** A multi-cycle bitmask fault injected repeatedly into multiplier outputs across consecutive butterfly invocations ($N = 100$).
4. **$F5B$ (Software Force-Value Zeroing):** An abstract software debug hook forcing intermediate addition/subtraction outputs to zero ($*t = 0$, $N = 100$).
5. **$H1$ (Harness Circular Verifier Evaluation):** Evaluation of verifier blindness in standalone test harnesses where checkers consume faulted live registers ($N = 600$ across 6 targets).
6. **$H2$ (Silent Data Corruption Evaluation):** End-to-end evaluation of shared key validity across all 300 active production fault trials ($100 \text{ F4} + 100 \text{ F5A} + 100 \text{ F5B}$, excluding 100 clean baseline controls), referenced alongside the 750 active fault trials of the canonical campaign.

### 5.3 Independent Correctness Oracles

To prevent self-referential validation:
- Valid decapsulations were compared against an independent clean-room ML-KEM-768 baseline ($K_{\text{valid}}$).
- Implicit rejections were validated against a standalone FIPS 203 Section 7.3 oracle recomputing $\text{SHAKE256}(z \parallel c, 32)$ directly using Keccak-f[1600].
- Binomial confidence intervals for production trials ($N = 300$) are reported as asymptotic 95% Wilson score confidence intervals [R03]. For the 750-trial canonical campaign, coverage is reported as an exact one-sided 97.5% Clopper-Pearson upper confidence bound.

